body:
@BYK Yes, @joakimbeng had not mentioned computed properties. But I check similar problems in verifying new issue. I believe it's a good practice.
In this case, computed properties is warned by this rule, it's a bug completely.

Also, I believe the shorthand properties case is a bug. That is [IdentifierReference, not PropertyName](http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/6.0/#sec-object-initializer).

@BYK 

> you also say object-shorthand "no matter what, make sure if I can write a property without quotes, don't quote them".

That's not what that rule is for. That rule says that if you can use the object-shorthand syntax you should use it, and it says nothing about quotes.

@BYK My intent is "This rule should ignore ~~computed~~/shorthand properties."

```
/* eslint-env es6 */
/* eslint quote-props: [2, "consistent"] */
var x;
var obj1 = ({
    "a-b": 0,
    x
});
var obj2 = ({
    x,
    "a-b": 0
});
```

```
> eslint test.js --no-eslintrc

test.js
  4:13  error  Inconsistently quoted property `x` found    quote-props
  8:13  error  Inconsistently quoted property `a-b` found  quote-props

✖ 2 problems (2 errors, 0 warnings)
```

I thought this is an unexpected behavior. Because `x` is an [IdentifierReference, not PropertyName](http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/6.0/#sec-object-initializer).
I expected no errors.
